1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A grammar is a recipe: each rule says how one symbol may be rewritten.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The right side may be any length: one letter, two symbols, or a whole string.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
A normal form removes that freedom: every rule must have one of two shapes.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Four passes get you there, and each pass is a small, checkable rewrite.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Chomsky normal form: A gives B C, or A gives a single terminal a.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Greibach normal form: A gives a, followed by any number of nonterminals.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The binary shape makes the parse tree a binary tree; that is the counting machine.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Neither form is a language class: every context-free grammar without the empty string has both.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A nonterminal is productive if some derivation from it ends in terminals.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
A nonterminal is reachable if it appears in some derivation from the start symbol.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Useless means one failure or the other: unreachable, or unproductive.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Five nonterminals become three, and the language does not move.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A nullable nonterminal is one that can vanish: it derives the empty string.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
For every rule, add a copy for each way its nullable symbols could vanish.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
One empty rule survives when the empty string is in the language.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The empty production can also create unit productions, which is why the next pass exists.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A unit production is a rule with one nonterminal and nothing else.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Compute the unit pairs by closure, then copy every non-unit rule along each pair.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
S now has four rules instead of three, and B keeps its own two.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A unit rule is not an error to leave in: it is a step that no counting theorem allows.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Here is a five-symbol grammar where every symbol is useful at the start.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Delete the empty rule and D, C and B stop producing anything.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
So the useless-symbol pass runs last of the three, never first.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Run the pass twice and a unit rule survives: S gives A, which the unit pass already passed.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Two shapes remain illegal: a terminal beside another symbol, and three symbols in a row.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Give each terminal its own nonterminal, and reuse it whenever the terminal recurs.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
A long right side becomes a chain: each new symbol holds two of the remaining symbols.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Isolate first, then split: the other order creates helpers for symbols you then move.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
One grammar, four passes, and a table of what each pass changed.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The final grammar has ten rules, every one of them of an allowed shape.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The check that matters: both grammars accept the same strings up to length six.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
On the textbook grammar the same code gives the same language on 120 strings.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A string of length n takes exactly 2n minus 1 steps in normal form.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The tree has 2n minus 1 internal nodes and 3n minus 1 nodes in total.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
With k nonterminals, a nonempty language has a string of length at most 2 to the k minus 1.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
GATE CSE 2024 Set 1 asked for the step count with ten variables; the answer is 179.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Membership becomes a triangle of cells: each cell asks which symbols derive that piece.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Each cell tries every split point, so the whole table costs n cubed.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Four question shapes: the step count, the validity check, the conversion, the bound.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Read the grammar, not the story: the shape decides the count, and the count decides the answer.

